; A `.cbor` payload applied INLINE to a target that is itself a `.cbor` payload
; (`bytes .cbor (bytes .cbor uint)`), in MEMBER position. Rejected gracefully.
;
; The serialize walk names a payload's staging buffer after the OWNING variable
; (`<var>_inner_se`), so both depths of one encoding chain mint the same binding: the outer write
; borrows what the inner `finalize()` moved. This generated at exit 0 with empty stderr and emitted
; a crate failing E0382 once per extra depth — the exact exit-0-uncompilable class the honesty
; invariants exist to make loud. Depth-threading that buffer name is what would make the shape
; generate, and is not implemented; refusing keys on exactly the broken set.
;
; Member position is deliberate: the refusal keys on the composition, not on the position, and the
; rule-BODY spelling of the same INLINE composition (`b = bytes .cbor (bytes .cbor uint)`) is
; refused identically — with the rule name prefixed, which is the only difference between the two
; seams.
;
; What stays supported is nesting a payload through something that owns its own serialize fn and
; therefore its own buffer: a NAMED payload (`inner = bytes .cbor uint` referenced as
; `bytes .cbor inner` — a `.cbor` rule body mints a wrapper struct, so the boundary is there with or
; without `@newtype`; tests/robustness/cbor_payload_same_chain_alias.cddl is that control) and a
; payload inside a payload's COLLECTION (tests/corpus/cbor_payload_nested.cddl). The refusal keys on
; two `CBORBytes` in ONE `RustType`'s chain so neither is touched.
foo = [b: bytes .cbor (bytes .cbor uint)]
